WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:06.000
In this work we present a quantum fuzzy controller for obstacle avoidance and goal directed navigation.

00:06.000 --> 00:12.000
Here the rover navigates in our indoor arena, avoiding obstacles and visiting a sequence of waypoints.

00:12.000 --> 00:18.000
The behavior you see is produced by an onboard lookup table based controller derived from offline quantum characterization.

00:18.000 --> 00:21.000
The controller is decoupled into two inference engines.

00:21.000 --> 00:25.000
One outputs linear velocity V, and the other outputs angular velocity omega.

00:25.000 --> 00:29.000
Overall, the navigation policy is driven by three perceptual variables:

00:29.000 --> 00:33.000
distance to goal error, orientation error, and obstacle bearing.

00:33.000 --> 00:37.000
The obstacle information is extracted from a gated forward-looking region.

00:37.000 --> 00:41.000
When the region is empty, the controller receives an explicit no-obstacle condition.

00:41.000 --> 00:44.000
The rule base is designed to be interpretable and safety-oriented.

00:44.000 --> 00:47.000
The limited number of fuzzy sets and rules reduces circuit width and depth,

00:47.000 --> 00:51.000
helping keep the overall design NISQ feasible and more resilient to noise.

00:52.000 --> 00:55.000
We implement the fuzzy controller with a quantum fuzzy inference engine,

00:55.000 --> 00:59.000
compiling membership functions and rules into quantum circuits.

00:59.000 --> 01:03.000
With three linguistic terms per variable, each inference engine fits in seven logical qubits.

01:03.000 --> 01:06.000
Four for the inputs and three for a one-hot output register.

01:07.000 --> 01:11.000
To run this controller on a robot, we cannot rely on cloud QPU calls inside the feedback loop.

01:12.000 --> 01:16.000
A remote call includes queuing, network transfer, circuit execution, and IO overhead,

01:16.000 --> 01:23.000
resulting in seconds of latency per control query, enough to block many control cycles at typical update rates.

01:24.000 --> 01:25.000
For this reason, we keep the quantum component offline.

01:25.000 --> 01:31.000
We use multiple quantum executions to sample the control surfaces, then deploy it online as a constant-time surrogate.

01:32.000 --> 01:39.000
Concretely, we precompute two lookup tables and evaluate actions online through lightweight interpolation at a fixed controller rate.

01:39.000 --> 01:43.000
To make offline characterization feasible within IBM QPU free access budgets,

01:43.000 --> 01:46.000
we use a gradient-based adaptive sampling strategy.

01:46.000 --> 01:50.000
This cuts the required quantum queries from 1800 to 62,

01:50.000 --> 01:54.000
while preserving the switching topology of the control maps needed for reliable deployment.

01:55.000 --> 01:58.000
We validate performance first in simulation across seven obstacle layouts,

01:58.000 --> 02:01.000
with two waypoint sets each, for a total of 14 missions.

02:02.000 --> 02:07.000
Each mission requires the rover to reach three waypoints in sequence and then return home, with a five centimeter tolerance.

02:08.000 --> 02:11.000
The controller succeeds in 12 of the 14 missions.

02:11.000 --> 02:18.000
The same overall outcome is observed whether the tables come from a noisy simulator or from a real quantum backend, IBM Torino.

02:18.000 --> 02:25.000
Beyond success rate, trajectories obtained using tables from the noisy simulator and from the real quantum backend remain largely overlapping.

02:26.000 --> 02:35.000
When differences appear, they mainly reflect benign local passage choices rather than a qualitative change in control logic.

02:36.000 --> 02:42.000
The tests also show two typical failure patterns: oscillations when a goal sits near an obstacle, and getting trapped in sharp corners.

02:43.000 --> 02:50.000
After simulation, we deploy the same control stack on a real four-wheeled rover in a 5 by 6 meter indoor laboratory arena.

02:50.000 --> 02:54.000
We use waypoint sequences drawn from four candidate locations with a randomized visiting order.

02:55.000 --> 03:02.000
We run multiple static trials, tighter docking with a 2.5 centimeter tolerance, and a dynamic obstacle injection stress test.

03:02.000 --> 03:08.000
In summary, we design and validate a quantum algorithm for reactive robot navigation using a quantum fuzzy inference engine.

03:08.000 --> 03:13.000
We preserve an interpretable auditable fuzzy rule base while implementing inference in a quantum circuit.

03:13.000 --> 03:19.000
The core motivation is QFIE's provable exponential advantage as inputs and linguistic resolution grow.

03:19.000 --> 03:24.000
Rather than claiming immediate runtime superiority in this intentionally low dimensional case study,

03:24.000 --> 03:30.000
we establish a concrete engineering pathway for quantum-derived inference under NISQ constraints.
